<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-deer">
  <title>When do road accidents with deer happen in Bavaria?</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        "But like other Bruegels, the painting is in part about details that hide in plain sight. If you didn't know
        the title, you might not notice Icarus at all."
      </p>
      <p>
        --- E. H. Gombrich on <em>Landscape with the Fall of Icarus</em>
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      <em>Background.</em> In parts of Europe, vehicle accidents involving deer are a serious road-safety issue.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> How many accidents involving deer occur? Are they evenly distributed through the year? At
      what times of day is risk highest?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> Hothorn et al. data and analyses.</p>
    <p>
      <em>Structure.</em> Two data frames of 172,596 observations each, with half-hourly accident counts in Bavaria:
      one for deer-involved accidents and one for other accidents.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-deer-accidents">
    <title>Vehicle accidents involving deer</title>

    <p>
      The deer-accident series spans 2002 to 2011. A smooth of daily counts shows a substantial long-run increase.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-deer1">
      <image source="images/deer1-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Smoothed daily numbers of deer-vehicle accidents in Bavaria, 2002-2011.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Monthly subseries displays indicate both an overall rise and possible seasonality.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-deer2">
      <image source="images/deer2-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Monthly deer-vehicle accident counts across the ten years.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Overlaying annual smooths reveals a recurring within-year pattern with two broad peaks.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-deer3">
      <image source="images/deer3-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Overlaid annual patterns of deer-vehicle accidents from January to December.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Weekly analysis suggests different seasonal behavior for weekdays versus weekends.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-deer4">
      <image source="images/deer4-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Day-of-week patterns in deer-vehicle accidents.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Half-hourly data show strong daily structure whose shape varies through the year; sunrise and sunset movement
      patterns align with the observed peaks.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-deer6">
      <image source="images/deer6-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Average daily deer-accident patterns by month (coloured) and year.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-deer7">
      <image source="images/deer7-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Daily deer-accident patterns by month across years (coloured by year).</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Comparing facet choices shows the month-faceted form more clearly exposes shifts in peak spacing across seasons.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-deer-nondeer">
    <title>What about vehicle accidents without deer?</title>

    <p>
      Non-deer accident data over the same decade provide a useful baseline for identifying deer-specific temporal
      features.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-veh1">
      <image source="images/veh1-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Smoothed daily non-deer accidents (blue) and deer accidents (brown), 2002-2011.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Non-deer accidents decline slightly overall and exhibit weaker seasonality and weekly variation than
      deer-involved accidents.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-veh2">
      <image source="images/veh2-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Daily non-deer accident patterns by month across years.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The non-deer daily profile is characterized by elevated daytime levels and lower overnight levels rather than
      strong dawn/dusk peaks.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-veh3">
      <image source="images/veh3-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Tuesday-only daily non-deer accident patterns across years.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-veh4">
      <image source="images/veh4-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Tuesday-only non-deer accident patterns for 2008, 2009, and 2010.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Close overlap in selected years suggests stable within-day structure, possibly linked to data-generation or
      reporting processes.
    </p>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <em>Answers.</em> Deer-vehicle accidents increased across the decade while other accidents declined slightly.
        Deer accidents show clear seasonal, weekly, and daily structure, with elevated risk around sunrise and sunset,
        especially in May, June, and November.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Further questions.</em> What mechanisms explain the detailed daily pattern in non-deer accidents?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-deer-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Time-axis label placement should be explicit and consistent in time-series displays.</p></li>
        <li><p>Smoothing can effectively reveal trends and recurring temporal structure.</p></li>
        <li><p>Facet-variable choice strongly affects interpretability.</p></li>
        <li><p>Overlaying series is useful for assessing similarity and stability.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
